        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>First Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan I)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>&#x27;But the beasts came roaring and were unleashed upon them; and when they reached the martyrs, THEY BOWED DOWN TO THEM AND PAID THEM HOMAGE&#x27; — the BEASTS-BOW-DOWN topos, with the strongest precedent in DANIEL 6:22 (the lions and Daniel) and recurring through Christian martyr-acts (Acts of Paul and Thecla — Thecla preserved by a lioness; Acts of Thomas; Martyrdom of Polycarp). Meqabyan&#x27;s specific count of SEVENTY-FIVE soldiers killed by the released beasts (3:26) is characteristic of the book&#x27;s love of numbered enumerations and has no obvious source. The verse establishes the divine-intervention pattern: beasts function as instruments of judgment-reversal, refusing the king&#x27;s command and executing the king&#x27;s soldiers instead.</p></aside>',
