        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>First Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan I)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>&#x27;And angels received them — the very men who had cast them in seeing it. THEIR SOULS THEY TOOK TO WHERE ABRAHAM AND ISAAC AND JACOB ARE — TO PARADISE WHERE JOY IS FOUND&#x27; — angels-receive-souls-to-Paradise martyrdom-completion. The Abraham-Isaac-Jacob destination is the bosom-of-Abraham paradise-locus (Luke 16:22 &#x27;carried by the angels into Abraham&#x27;s bosom&#x27;); the triple-patriarch is the standard EOTC formula for the righteous-dead&#x27;s abode (cf. seed 2:22 invocation). The verse closes the 1 Mq Ch 3 martyrdom-cycle with the canonical angelic-psychopomp motif (parallels Apocalypse of Paul §§14-18 soul-at-death narrative; Testament of Asher 1:3-9). The destination&#x27;s name-form (&#x27;where joy is found,&#x27; ደስታ ስፍራ) is a distinctive Tewahedo Paradise-vocabulary.</p></aside>',
