        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>First Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan I)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>&#x27;When that criminal saw that they were dead, he commanded that their FLESH BE BURNED WITH FIRE until they became ash, and that the wind scatter them. But the fire from morning till evening was kindled and did not touch them&#x27; — the CORPSES-RESIST-DESTRUCTION motif. Fire (4:1-2) → sea-and-millstones (4:3) → beasts-and-carrion-birds (4:4-5) → all fail to harm the martyrs&#x27; bodies. The motif parallels the THREE-YOUNG-MEN-IN-THE-FIERY-FURNACE narrative (Daniel 3:19-27 — fire cannot harm Shadrach + Meshach + Abednego) + the JONAH-IN-THE-FISH preservation (Jonah 1:17 + 2:10) + Christian-martyr-acts hagiographic tradition of incorruptible relics (Polycarp&#x27;s body found unburned in the flames; Eulalia&#x27;s incorruptible body in Prudentius Peristephanon 3). The Meqabyan version compresses the four-fold elemental-failure (fire/water/land-beast/sky-bird) into one chapter as a complete-cosmos resistance.</p></aside>',
