        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>First Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan I)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>&#x27;They did so; but the beasts and the carrion-birds did not touch their corpses. CARRION-BIRDS AND BIRDS OF THE AIR COVERED THEM WITH THEIR WINGS, that they might be a covering for the holy ones&#x27; — divine-protection-by-wings continuation of the corpses-resist-destruction motif. The bird-protection motif extends Psalm 91:4 (&#x27;he shall cover thee with his feathers, and under his wings shalt thou trust&#x27;) from the living to the martyred-dead. Tewahedo iconography of the saint-protected-by-divine-bird-wings (found across Aksumite manuscript-illumination) is rooted in this verse-tradition. The chapter closes (4:6ff.) with angels joining the bird-protection and the corpses being preserved for forty days before being taken to Paradise — completing the martyr-cult preservation cycle that the Tewahedo Sǝnkǝsar (Synaxarium) recapitulates for every commemorated saint.</p></aside>',
