        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>First Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan I)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>NIMROD — proud-tower-builder, humbled by God. The Genesis 11:1-9 Tower-of-Babel narrative names Nimrod (Gen 10:8-12) as the founder-of-Babel; Meqabyan integrates the Nimrod-tower tradition with the broader proud-king-humbled motif. The Nimrod-as-pride-exemplar reading parallels Josephus Antiquities 1.4.2 (Nimrod&#x27;s tower &#x27;designed to gain immortality and challenge God&#x27;); 3 Baruch 2-3 (the tower-builders punished); Pseudo-Philo Liber Antiquitatum Biblicarum 4.7 + 6.13 (the tower-builders dispersed). The verse establishes the second-fold in the chapter&#x27;s catalog of humbled-kings (Re&#x27;aytawi-likely-Pharaoh in seed 5:1 → Nimrod-the-tower-builder → Nebuchadnezzar-humbled-to-beasts at 5:14) — a typological indictment of Ṣiruṣaydan&#x27;s pride.</p></aside>',
