        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>First Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan I)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>NEBUCHADNEZZAR humbled-to-beasts — Daniel 4 directly cited. The Babylonian king&#x27;s seven-year humiliation (&#x27;he was driven from men, and did eat grass as oxen, and his body was wet with the dew of heaven, till his hairs were grown like eagles&#x27; feathers, and his nails like birds&#x27; claws&#x27; — Dan 4:33) becomes the climactic exemplar of God&#x27;s-judgment-on-proud-kings. The verse completes the chapter&#x27;s tripartite humbled-king catalog and locates Ṣiruṣaydan&#x27;s pride within the recurring biblical pattern. Meqabyan&#x27;s distinctive contribution: the catalog-form of humbled-kings serves as PROPHETIC-WARNING addressed-to-Ṣiruṣaydan via the brothers&#x27; homiletic voice; the prophet-confronts-king pattern (Daniel-confronting-Nebuchadnezzar in Daniel 2 + Daniel 4) is preserved in this Meqabyan recapitulation.</p></aside>',
