        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>First Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan I)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>Saul-Samuel-Amalek pericope (vv. 20-38) — 1 Samuel 15 obedience-over-sacrifice anchor. Samuel&#x27;s confrontation of Saul (&#x27;hath the LORD as great delight in burnt offerings and sacrifices, as in obeying the voice of the LORD? Behold, to obey is better than sacrifice, and to hearken than the fat of rams&#x27; — 1 Sam 15:22) is the canonical OBEDIENCE-OVER-SACRIFICE formula that Meqabyan recapitulates. The Meqabyan brothers&#x27; refusal to sacrifice-to-idols (the standard Meqabyan martyrology cause) is here grounded in the Samuel-Saul precedent: SACRIFICE WITHOUT OBEDIENCE IS REJECTION; OBEDIENCE WITHOUT SACRIFICE IS ACCEPTANCE. Parallels Hosea 6:6 (&#x27;I desired mercy, and not sacrifice; and the knowledge of God more than burnt offerings&#x27;), Matthew 9:13 + 12:7 (Christ citing Hosea against the Pharisees), and Hebrews 10:5-9 (&#x27;Sacrifice and offering thou wouldest not, but a body hast thou prepared me&#x27;). The chapter unifies royal-faithfulness (vv. 1-18) with obedience-not-sacrifice (vv. 20-38) as the integrated Meqabyan royal-theology.</p></aside>',
