        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>First Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan I)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>OPENS Ch 7: direct SECOND-PERSON ADDRESS TO THE KING on the duties of royal office. The chapter&#x27;s homiletic mode shifts from third-person narrative (chs 2-6) to direct apostrophe — Ṣiruṣaydan is addressed YOU. The argumentation moves from the king&#x27;s CREATEDNESS (vv. 1-5) to the duty of IMPARTIAL JUDGMENT (vv. 6-14) to the DIVINE MULTIPLICATION OR SHORTENING OF DAYS according to obedience (v. 15). The direct-address-to-king genre parallels: Wisdom of Solomon 6:1-11 (&#x27;Hear therefore, O ye kings, and understand; learn, ye that be judges of the ends of the earth&#x27;); Sirach 10:1-11 (&#x27;A wise judge will instruct his people&#x27;); Romans 13:1-7 (Paul on the ruler bearing the sword); 1 Peter 2:13-17 (Petrine on submission to civil-authorities). Meqabyan&#x27;s distinctive feature: the brothers themselves voice the address, not a prophet or sage — making the martyrs into the prophetic-instructors of the king who is destroying them.</p></aside>',
