        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>First Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan I)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>Seed-buried-and-rising argument — the STRONGEST PAULINE PARALLEL to 1 Corinthians 15:36-38 in the entire Meqabyan trilogy (per CROSS_REFERENCE_APPENDIX §10). Paul&#x27;s argument: &#x27;thou fool, that which thou sowest is not quickened, except it die: and that which thou sowest, thou sowest not that body that shall be, but bare grain... but God giveth it a body as it hath pleased him, and to every seed his own body.&#x27; Meqabyan 8:22-32 develops the same seed-buried argument: the seed dies in burial → emerges with a transformed body → so the dead rise with transformed bodies. The parallel is THEOLOGICALLY DENSE: both are anti-Sadducean / anti-Sectarian resurrection-polemic; both ground the resurrection in everyday agricultural observation; both insist on continuity-of-identity-through-transformation-of-form. The Meqabyan-Pauline parallel is THE STRONGEST single anchor for Meqabyan&#x27;s reception-history in the early Christian resurrection-doctrine tradition.</p></aside>',
