        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>First Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan I)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>OPENS Ch 11: &#x27;HERMON AND HER WALLS HAVE FALLEN; for God has repaid them according to their wickedness&#x27; — the chapter explicitly names TYRE AND SIDON as the typological-source for Ṣiruṣaydan&#x27;s name. Per Horovitz 1905 p. 195 fn. 3 + Dillmann LEXICON LINGUAE AETHIOPICAE (1865, the originator of the etymology): **Ṣiruṣaydan = Ṣir-uṣ-Aydan = TYRE + SIDON** (the Phoenician-coastal cities, joined by the conjunction). Tyre + Sidon are the canonical BIBLICAL-TYPE for arrogant maritime-commercial pride (Ezekiel 26-28 prophecies; Isaiah 23 burden-of-Tyre; Joel 3:4-8). The villain-name is a TYPOLOGICAL CIPHER for the arrogant-imperial-city-state rather than historical-king identification. The chapter prophesies Tyre+Sidon&#x27;s eschatological-shame at resurrection (11:3 — &#x27;at the resurrection, the shame of Jerusalem shall be revealed&#x27;); Jerusalem and Tyre+Sidon are both judged by the same divine-criterion (covenant-faithfulness).</p></aside>',
