        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>First Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan I)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>OPENS Ch 19: CAIN&#x27;S INVENTION OF MUSICAL INSTRUMENTS — direct expansion of Genesis 4:21 (&#x27;and his brother&#x27;s name was Jubal: he was the father of all such as handle the harp and organ&#x27; — actually Cain&#x27;s descendant Jubal, but Meqabyan attributes the invention to Cain directly). Parallels JUBILEES 4:17-22 (Jubilees attributes musical-and-craft inventions to specific Cainite + Sethite descendants in detailed genealogy) + 1 Enoch 8 (the Watchers as teachers of forbidden arts including ornaments and instruments). Meqabyan&#x27;s distinctive contribution: Cain himself (not just his descendants) is the musical-instrument inventor, framing musical-culture as morally-ambiguous (originating in the Cain-line alongside violence-and-deceit). The chapter also includes the extra-canonical tradition that Abel had a beautiful wife whom Cain killed him to obtain (v. 2; paralleled in Conflict of Adam and Eve with Satan).</p></aside>',
